course: everyday
lesson: 357
title: Snooping on phones, diaries and letters
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Ruby in Perth watched her big brother type his phone code and later unlocked it to look at his games and messages. He saw his apps had moved and asked who had been on it. She owned up; he changed his code and would not lend her anything for weeks. She promised not to watch people type their codes again.
explain: Three weeks later, bored on a wet afternoon, Ruby did it again with Mum's tablet. This time she found the plans for her own birthday trip and spoiled it. The guilt was worse than the first time, because she had promised. She told Mum, and Mum said: «Everyone slips. I forgive you. But a habit changes slowly — so when you're bored, come and find me, and we'll find you something to do.» Ruby put a list of rainy-day things on the fridge.
explain: Knowing someone's code is not the same as being allowed to use it. When a friend hands you his phone to play a game, he trusts you with the game, not with his messages.
explain: Other snoops end the same way. Grace looked in her friend's bag while he was at the toilet; he saw her, was upset and did not want to sit with her. Ava read Mum's emails and found out about a surprise trip: the surprise was gone, and so was Mum's trust for a while. Dad caught Ben at the keyhole looking at hidden presents and simply moved them somewhere else.
explain: Each slip back needs the same honest steps — own up, say sorry, put it right — and a plan to make the next slip less likely.
fact: Knowing a password or code does not give you permission to use it.
fact: Watching someone type their code so you can use it later is a kind of snooping.
fact: When someone lends you their phone for one thing, it is only for that thing.
fact: Listening in on someone else's phone call is snooping, even inside your own home.
fact: A bag is private; looking in it while the owner is away is snooping.
fact: Report cards and important letters are usually meant for parents to read first.
fact: Reading the end of a story first often spoils the fun of the waiting.
fact: Slipping back into a bad habit after a promise is common; changing habits takes many tries.
fact: A plan for the moment of temptation, such as a list of things to do when bored, helps habits change.
fact: Forgiveness after a slip does not mean you can repeat it; each time still needs putting right.
exercise: How did Ruby get into her brother's phone? | She had watched him type his code | He gave her permission to use it whenever she liked | The phone had no lock | She guessed his birthday
exercise: Ruby knew the code. Did that mean she was allowed to use the phone? | No, a code isn't permission | Yes, if you know the code you are allowed to use it | Yes, between brothers and sisters | Only for games, not messages
exercise: What was the consequence the first time? | Her brother changed his code and stopped lending her things | Her brother gave her his old phone | Nothing happened, because he never noticed a thing | She got a phone of her own
exercise: Why did Ruby feel worse the second time? | She had broken a promise, and spoiled her own surprise | She was caught by the police | The tablet broke | She had to give up her birthday party because of it
exercise: What did Mum say about slipping back? | Everyone slips; habits change slowly; make a plan | Ruby could never be trusted again, whatever she did | It does not matter if you say sorry | Slipping back is only for little children
exercise: What was Ruby's plan to stop snooping when bored? | A list of rainy-day things to do on the fridge | Hiding all the tablets herself | Asking Mum and Dad to buy her a tablet of her very own | Staying in bed when it rains
exercise: Theo's friend gave him his phone to play a game, and Theo read his texts. Why was that wrong? | He was trusted with the game, not the messages | Texts are always boring | His friend should have deleted the texts before lending it | It is fine if you don't reply
exercise: Theo read a message about himself and felt hurt. What does that show? | Snooping can hurt the snooper too | Friends always write nice things about each other | Texts should be read aloud | Phones should not have messages
exercise: Grace looked in her friend's bag while he went to the toilet. He saw her. What happened? | He was upset and did not want to sit with her | He offered her a sweet from the bag | He did not mind at all | He looked in her bag too, and they both just laughed about it
exercise: Jack listened to his sister's phone call on the other phone. She heard him breathing. What would put it right? | Saying sorry and hanging up straight away next time | Saying he was only checking that the phone line was working | Listening more quietly next time | Telling Mum what she said
exercise: Ella looked at her report card before her parents did. She was nervous all day. What was the wiser choice? | Give it to her parents and read it with them | Hide it until the holidays | Change the marks with a pen so nobody gets upset | Throw it away on the way home
exercise: Sam peeked at the last page of his new book. What happened to the story? | It wasn't exciting any more | It became even more exciting than before | The ending changed completely | Nothing, endings don't matter
exercise: Ava read Mum's emails and found out about a surprise trip. What did it cost? | The surprise, and Mum's trust for a while | Nothing, because the trip still felt exactly the same | The trip was cancelled forever | Mum's laptop broke
exercise: Dad caught Ben looking through the keyhole at hidden presents. What did Dad do? | He moved the presents somewhere else | He gave Ben all the presents early instead | He let Ben choose the wrapping paper himself | He laughed and showed him all of them
exercise: A girl got away with reading her sister's texts three times. On the fourth she was caught. What does this show? | Getting away with it before did not make it safe | It shows she was unlucky only once | It means the first three times were fine and only the fourth was wrong | It means texts should be hidden
exercise: Ruby slipped back once. Which is the wisest view of herself? | I slipped, I'll put it right and make a plan | I'm a bad person and I can't ever change | It doesn't matter since Mum forgives | I should never promise anything again
test: «saw my bro type his code, 2580, easy». You could get on his phone now. Best move? | Don't use it, and maybe tell him to change it | Use it once for a quick look and then put it back | Use it only for games | Write it down for later
test: mate hands u his phone to play Minecraft and a text pops up «don't tell him but—». What's right? | Stay in the game, don't open it | Open it, it's right there on the screen | Read it and tell him what it said later on | Screenshot it so you can read it later
test: «I promised not to snoop but I did it again, I'm useless». What's the wise reply? | Everyone slips; own up, put it right and make a plan | Yes, you're useless forever | Don't own up this time, it'll only make things worse | Promises don't matter anyway
test: Mum: «Somebody's been on my tablet and the birthday plans are open». It was you. Best reply? | «It was me, sorry. I spoiled the surprise» | «It just opened by itself on the screen» | «Probably a hacker» | «Did you leave it open?»
test: read my sister's texts 3 times and she never knew, «it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, it was never fine, and now it's a habit to stop | Yes, three times proves it's safe | Yes, sisters share everything | Only if you never tell anyone about it
test: big sis on the phone in the hall, other handset's right there. Best choice? | Leave it, her call is private | Pick it up very quietly so she won't hear | Listen in for just one minute only | Record it so you can listen later
test: report card in your bag, envelope says «To the parents of…». What's right? | Give it to your parents unopened | Open it carefully and stick it back down | Read it and hide it | Steam it open
test: «just read the last page, it's quicker». What do you lose? | The fun of waiting to find out | Nothing at all, the ending is the same | The first page | The book's cover
test: Priest: «Have you kept your promise from last time?» You haven't. What's honest? | Say no, and that you want to try again | Say yes, and then leave the church quickly | Change the subject | Say promises don't count to God
test: friend's bag's open on the desk while he's out, you can see a note with your name. Best move? | Leave it and ask him later if you want | Read the note quickly before he comes back | Take the note home | Hide his bag as a joke
test: Why might a list of things to do on rainy days help Ruby stop snooping? | It gives her something to do when the temptation comes | It hides the tablet from her so she can never find it | It makes her forget the codes | It means Mum checks on her
test: Ruby's brother changed his code and won't lend her stuff. She says «he's just being mean». What's closer to the truth? | It's a natural consequence of broken trust | He's mean for no reason | He just wants an excuse to get himself a new phone | Codes are changed every week
exam: Identify the principal reason for which knowledge of another person's access code does not entitle one to use the device. | Permission is granted by the owner and not by knowledge of the code | Codes are known only to their owners | Devices within a household may be used freely by any family member | Entitlement arises from proximity to the device
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a relapse into a habit previously renounced? | It is common and requires renewed amends and a practical plan | It demonstrates that change is impossible | It requires no further amends once forgiveness has been given | It cancels all prior efforts at improvement
exam: It is given that a child, entrusted with a friend's telephone for the purpose of a game, read that friend's messages. Select the correct characterisation. | The child exceeded the trust granted and acted wrongly | The child acted entirely within the permission which had been given | The friend is responsible for the intrusion | No wrong occurred as nothing was altered
exam: State the course of action most consistent with propriety in respect of a sealed school report addressed to a pupil's parents. | It is delivered unopened to the parents | It is opened and resealed by the pupil | It is retained by the pupil until the holidays | It is read aloud to classmates
